<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fashoda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kodok</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fashoda, now Kodok, is a town on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>. It was the royal centre of the Shilluk kingdom from about the 16th century. In 1898 a French expedition under Jean-Baptiste <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marchand-dessalines">Marchand</a> occupied it and was confronted by Kitchener's Anglo-Egyptian forces, a crisis that ended with French withdrawal and British control of the Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fashoda.jpg" alt="Fashoda" width="320" height="151" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fashoda_-_The_Governor%27s_House_(1906)_-_TIMEA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
